ecember. It was horrible on the ship, even though my sister and I always found kids to play with. And we adapted. It's amazing. Kids adapt to whatever it is. People don't think they do, but they do. And one day, in the morning, we looked out. And guess what was in front of us? The Statue of Liberty. I mean, it makes me want to cry, because it was unbelievable, unbelievable. The moment we saw it, we were in America. So--

INT: Do you know, your arrival date?

ER: Yes, January 14, in 1942.

INT: OK. OK. Was anyone waiting at the harbor for you?

ER: Yes. For one thing, we had people who-- what's it called? They gave affidavits for us.

INT: Yes.

ER: So this was a friend of my father's, Fred Pfeil. He was a photographer in New York. And an aunt was there, and maybe like three or four people. And the-- we-- it took us two days to get off the ship. Or one-- maybe one day, only, to get off the ship and to go through all the processing, medical exams and all kinds of things. And so we had to wait. And then we finally were taken to a hotel in Manhattan. And they brought us milk, which we hadn't had-- I mean, I think we both lost like half our weight coming over-- and sandwiches and things like that. And so we had our first meal in the hotel. And we were in Manhattan at that point.

INT: Do you remember if your mother had any money left?

ER: Well, my-- my-- as I said, my father was very proactive. He knew we wanted to get out. And when he found out in 1939 that he got his visa, he sent a gold brick to the United States. So that's what Mom used when we got here. Now, how much it was worth, I have no idea. I know she worked. She had to work, but I think that was the source of her money in the beginning.

INT: So when you arrived, you went to the hotel. And then where did you go from there?

ER: Oh, my aunt had a house with her husband and two adopted kids. She had a house in Orange, New Jersey.

INT: Mhm.

ER: And we went there for a few days. And then my mother was looking for an apartment, and she found an apartment. And here's the coincidences in the world. I mean, the coincidence-- I've skipped over many, but here's one. She went in. There was a landlady, and the landlady-- Mrs. Laval. And the landlady said, where are you from? And she said, I'm from Zagreb. She said, so am I. When they started talking, they realized that she had been in school with my grandmother, a landlady on 75th street between Madison and Park in New York. [LAUGHING] So that was very nice for my mom. So my mother rented furnished apartments, and that's where we stayed. I think-- I think they might have been one room, maybe one bedroom. And she did that for four years. Except, every summer, she would take all our stuff, put it in storage. And the next time when we came back-- because she wanted us out of New York in the summer because it was so hot. It was horrible. Before air conditioning, so it was hot. So we would move every single summer, come back, get another furnished apartment. But the school I went to, the first school, was PS 166 in-- on 88th Street in New York. And they put me in a C class. They called it a C class. I don't know what that stood for. No idea. It was a class for foreigners. I guess they thought that was a way to teach them English. Well, we spent 3/4 of the year speaking French, German, any language that people could communicate in. And everybody spoke German or French or something. So we never learned any English. So the next year, she took us out, and we moved to another place. And we went to a different school, PS 87. And there, they put me in third grade. Well, I think I was supposed to be in fourth grade. But, well, they put me like in second, third. It was combined. They pushed me through. And there, I learned. You had to speak English. I mean, everybody spoke English, and kids learn very, very quickly. And so I was looking at a diary that I kept in all those years. And the funny part is I-- my daughter actually found the page that says, "Dear Diary, I'm going to be writing to you today, but I will write in English." And that's dated 1941. No, no, no, no, no. 1943. One year I was here, and I wrote a diary from then on in English, which is-- kids learn fast. My mother came to school, and the teacher talked to her. And she said, what-- maybe we were here maybe a year and a half by that time. She said, where are you from? She says, I'm from Yugoslavia. And she said, but your kids were born here. So that's what happens. [LAUGHING] So that was what happened when we got to New York.

INT: OK. And how many family members did you have here? Were there any?

ER: We had-- my father, as I told you, tried to get out of Yugoslavia. But his sister and her husband and two boys, the year before, were able to leave.

INT: Can you tell me their names?

ER: Their name is Alice, [? Vita ?] and Paulie, Paul [? Vita. ?] The two boys were Peter and Tommy. And they moved to Los Angeles. My uncle was a OBGYN doctor. So he did-- they did very well in Los Angeles. And so they were here, but we didn't talk to them. They were in Los Angeles, so we had no contact. In New York, we had a lot of people whose maybe parents or grandparents were cousins. So they were like third cousins removed or, you know, distant people. But when you-- well, we lost everybody. I'll tell you how we found that out. But, you know, you get close to whatever family is left. [LAUGHING] So we were very close to them. My aunt, Tante Mitzi, only spoke German, and so we renewed our German, and she took care of us when-- My mother studied all the time for her medical exams. She had to take all the medical exams, like medical students do, to get her licensing. And that took two years. Oh, she had to learn English first. And she had us to support, so she worked for the UN. And she learned English, and she had to take her exams. So-- difficult life.

INT: What did she do for the UN?

ER: Translation.

INT: OK.

ER: Was it-- was it the UN, then?

INT: No, it was after. After the war, it became--

ER: Yeah, it was United-- It was-- I don't remember the name.

INT: The predecessor.

ER: Yeah, it was one of those things.

INT: Can you tell me-- we touched very quickly on family members that were lost in Europe. Can you tell me any names?

ER: Yeah. So, as I told you, we moved every summer to get us out of New York. The first summer, we went to South Carolina to see some family. Actually, the husband was my godfather. They had a pepper-- red pepper farm. And they brought red pepper to the United States. Paprika. Sorry, not red pepper. Paprika to the United States. So we stayed there for a month. And the next year, we went to-- we started going to camp. And I don't know which year we were at camp when, in August, the war-- V-E Day. No, wait a minute. V-J Day was in New York? Yes. V-E day was before V-J Day. So victory in Europe in August, in camp. Then we went back to New York and went to other camp. And then when V-J Day came about, which was not long-- I don't know how long after that. Five months, I think. Yeah.

INT: Probably.

ER: After that, my mother got a telegram from an uncle in Zagreb, Uncle Dragutin in Zagreb. And the telegram in one-- or maybe it was a letter-- in one swoop gave her all the information about the family. Everybody that they mentioned died, was killed in a concentration camp. No, they didn't say was killed in a concentration camp. They said they died of typhus. That's how they used to talk about their death. Everybody died of typhus. Her mother, her two in-laws, her three sisters, brother, everybody else too, this-- and their families. One sister survived and tried to come to the United States later on. Her husband had escaped from a prisoner of war camp. And then they were in a concentration camp. And at some point-- and they, I guess, escaped or-- Anyway, they got out of those camps. And my mother was able to bring them to where they would take them. And the only place she could find is Venezuela. So they went to Caracas. And that's where my cousins, they had one boy-- first cousin. So he lived in Caracas. And that's the only sister who survived. Her brother and his wife were in a concentration camp. She got pregnant. Or she was pregnant and had a child. Her name was Renate-- had a baby. Renate--

INT: Let me clarify. She had the baby in the camp?

ER: She had the baby in the camp is what I heard.

INT: Mhm.

ER: They had best friends, the FÃ¼rsts. And the best friends, they snuck her out some way, the baby. And they took the baby and adopted Renate. The parents were killed in the concentration camps. So Renate lived with this family in Zagreb, until she went to Israel later on and married. So that was another long story. [LAUGHING] But I don't know what else to tell you.

INT: All right, so you've arrived in America. Your mother passes her exams, and you're going to school. Tell me about your school life then.

ER: OK. My school life was OK, on the West Side at PS 87. But then my mother passed her exam, and we moved to 86th Street, between Madison and Park, which was sort of a upscale kind of place, although our place was not upscale. What she did was she bought an office, all medical equipment from a doctor. And it was our apartment and office on the first floor of an apartment-- prewar apartment house.

INT: Mhm.

ER: So she started her office there, and we had our house, our apartment, in the same place. We had one bathroom, for the patients and for us. Her-- the living room turned into a waiting room during the day. And this was-- she stayed there until she died in '98-- at 98. But that's where we lived. Maya and I had our own bedroom next to the kitchen. And when we moved there, I went to PS 6, which was one of the best public schools in New York, in Manhattan or New York. And I was treated terribly. I used to come home crying every day. Because we couldn't afford clothing, I was wearing high shoes. Today, everybody wears high shoes. But it was, you know, like hiking shoes. Nobody wore hiking-- they all wore these-- What did they call them? I can't remember. They were white and brown.

INT: Mhm. Oxfords.

ER: Yeah, they wore different clothes. And then, I wore hand-me-downs. I don't really know, but they really made fun of us-- me. I mean, they were cruel. That's sixth grade. And I was put into sixth grade, which was-- I forgot what they called it, but it was-- they had one honors grade, you know, where kids were brighter or something. So they put me into that. So I guess by that time I was OK, but the kids were horrible to me. And it wasn't-- it wasn't antisemitism. New York was filled with Jews. So it was just that I was different. Until one day, three girls got together. They were going to do their-- their-- I don't know, Girl Scout duty or something. And they invited me to join them to play with them in the afternoon. It was sixth grade. And I went, and they found out that I was just a regular person. And they liked me. So after that, I-- you know, they had in-groups and out-groups. I'd been in the out-group. So then I became one of their friends. So then after that, things were OK. By that time, we were almost in seventh grade.

INT: How was your mother's English?

ER: My mother got-- she spoke very well, [PAUSES] had a very heavy accent. And the only thing that I think she did-- never really learned is the idioms. Like, she never understood jokes. But maybe that was just her lack of sense of humor. No, she was-- she had a great sense of humor. That's right. But, no, her English was perfect because, I mean, she was a doctor, and she learned English. She had to learn Spanish when she was in 86th Street because everybody was from Puerto Rico. She was-- she had rights at Mount Sinai. She had to give a day a week to work at Mount Sinai in the department. I have a picture of that, her with all the doctors. And so she, you know, spoke English all the time, and Spanish, if they happen to be Puerto Rican. And there were many then, in that area, in Harlem. So-- but my mother was-- she was a very upbeat person. When we were in Berlin, for instance, this-- we'd go to the cellar every night, almost, because bombs were falling. It was before America was in the war. So the English were bombing Berlin. And every morning, we'd get up, and there'd be some other building that's not there. And we were right next to the zoo. So one day, we went to the zoo. The next day, we woke up, and it was not there anymore. And my mother would whisper to us, don't talk to anybody here, but we're very happy that they're bombing. This is good for us. And she kept telling us how good things were. And she'd keep us playing games and knitting. And, you know, she was just always so upbeat, to us. I'm sure she was pretty sad and depressed. I don't know. But she made things very pleasant, even under those sort of circumstances. So when she came to the United States, she adapted very, very quickly and was really loved by most people. She never, never was bitter, even with all the news about her family, with-- I never understood it. She was-- she just didn't think about it.

INT: OK. And did you make friends? Did you have girlfriends? Did you go to the movies?

ER: I had-- no, I didn't go to the movies. No, I went to the movies once when I was-- every weekend, my sister and I went-- the first four years we were there, when my mother was studying, we went-- Maya and I got on a subway down to the Battery, took a ferry over to New Jersey, took a bus, when we got to the ferry, to go to my aunt's for the weekend. So that my mother could work and study. And so I didn't make any friends then. I was just too busy working. After this incident with these girlfriends who decided they liked me, that I was OK, so I became popular and went-- eventually, ended up having very good friends. We had a group of friends, one of whom I'm still in touch with-- every five years, though. And then I went to Bronx High School of Science. And--

INT: How did you make that choice?

ER: I wanted to be a doctor, physician. So I went there, and science was much easier than, for me, than history, which I didn't know what was about, or English, I mean, any other subject. Science came very naturally to me. I liked it. I was good in math. And so I went to be a doctor. And it was-- there were only four-- [PAUSES] one-- three good schools in New York. So Stuyvesant was a boys' school. My eventual husband went there. Hunter was a girls' school. I didn't want just a girls' school. So Bronx Science seemed like the logical place.

INT: How many girls were in your class, let's say?

ER: Oh, my god, it was the fourth class of girls. And at that time, I-- they asked me to run for office. There'd never been a girl running for office there. And I did run for office, despite how nervous and scared I was. I mean, I was-- it was really hard for me, but there was a young man who said, well, he'll help me, he'll help me. And he did. And I became secretary of the student body in the school. So by that point, I was really in good shape. You know, I was happy, happy and worked hard. I only worked hard because I needed to-- I was used to it. I had to take care of my sister from the time we came to the America-- to America till I went to-- not-- Yeah, even when I went-- till I went to college, probably. She was-- she-- when we first came, she had to go with me every minute. She could not be alone. So she had to-- so I made a deal with her that she should go with my friends twice, and I'll go with her friends once, because my friends are so much more interesting at that age. So she did that. She had to do it. And then she would go home. This is so funny. I think she told this story, but she would go home from kindergarten, and I went back to school. We went home for lunch and then went back to school for the afternoon session. She would have-- she'd go home, and there was only a morning session. She had to lie on my mother's bed till I came home at 3:00. [PAUSES] And she did. She could take a doll with her or whatever she wanted, but she had to stay on the bed, except to go to the bathroom. But she did that, and I came home. And then, my mother would, I suppose, prepare some food and make dinner. And my mother wouldn't come back till late at night. So I really took care of everything from then on. And I was, [PAUSES] I think, eight, nine. So anyway, so my sister and I didn't have many friends. But I had many more than she did because we went with them.

INT: OK. Then you graduated from high school?

ER: I graduated from high school, went to Cornell for college. When I was a freshman in high school, I met a guy through a long story. My mother was a doctor at Mount Sinai, and his mother was a pediatrician in Mount Sinai, too. And so I used to go square dancing, and I met him there. I mean, like a parallel thing. His mother passed away. And so then, his name was Eddie Ray. That's still my last name. And he was-- went to Cornell two years before me. He was-- and I went to Cornell, partially because it was a very good school, and a big part was because he was there. After the freshman year, we got married, my freshman year. And he stayed on to get a master's so I could finish. And I graduated. And instead of graduating in the graduation, I had a baby. So Jude was born-- this-- [POINTING] June, instead of my graduating, my mother said, come home. You're expecting in two weeks. Don't-- I had to drive home. So anyway, so then after Cornell, I had Jude. And then my husband had to go in the Air Force because he was ROTC. So we went to Wright-Patterson, Ohio, for two years. And while we were there, I had twins. So the twins-- Jude was 21 months old when I had the twins. And then, we moved to Cleveland because he got a job in Cleveland after the Air Force. And I had another child. So within four years, I had-- within three years, I had four children.

INT: And a college degree.

ER: And a college degree. And so then, we were in Cleveland. I went to Western Reserve, it was called at the time. Now, it's Case Western. But I went just at night. I took one course at night. And he would stay home with the kids when I went to the course. And then, we moved. He was called by GE to solve some problems that he was sort of an expert in. And we moved here to Villanova, in this house. And when-- and then, I wanted to find a Jewish preschool. And there were no Jews around here, no Democrats around here. Nobody younger than, like, 30. I don't know. I was 27 when I moved here. And I had four kids, and I had to drive them. Then, I had to drive every day to preschool, because I wanted her to have a Jewish education. Because I didn't know enough to give it to her. My sister went to Hebrew school, but we couldn't afford for me to go. They wouldn't let us go in those days. It was just terrible. And I had one course for my confirmation. That was with Rabbi Milton Steinberg. You may not know who he is, but he was a very famous rabbi. And it was a seminar course at his house. And to this-- it's the only formal Jewish education, except courses I might have taken later. But I almost remember everything about that course. It was such a marvelous, marvelous experience. Anyway, so the kids [PAUSES] started elementary school. Jeanne started preschool. No, Jude was in first grade. Sorry. Because we were in Atlantic City, waiting to find a house here. And she started first grade in Atlantic City, in Ventnor. And then we moved here. She was in first grade, and the twins and Jean went to the preschool at the synagogue. And I drove them every day and so on. And then, when Jean was three, I went back to school. Because I wanted to get-- at that point, I had done so much research at Cornell. I was an honors student, and they told me I was so bright, and it was-- research was the thing to do and not medicine, blah, blah, blah. So I got talked out of medicine pretty well, and I decided to get a PhD. And so that's what I did then, at Bryn Mawr College, which is about three miles from here. But the reason I went there is because the guru in the field I wanted to go into was there. So I was going to work with her, and I ended up not.

So-- [LAUGHING]

INT: OK.

ER: Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

INT: OK, thank you.

ER: School? Yeah.

INT: Yes. You attended graduate school.

ER: I did.

INT: You had four children. And you-- your husband was here and going to work. It sounds like your typical American life. Did you feel like a typical American?

ER: This was completely atypical for my era.

INT: Yeah.

ER: There were no mothers around here who worked. They used to tell my kids that I was a bad mother because-- I might have been much less of a helicopter mother, but I think I was a good mother because I loved my kids. And we spent every summer, for instance, together. We did wonderful things in the summers. And they didn't-- they only went to camp once or twice, so we could be together. But as I said, there were-- women did not work in those days. But of course, at Bryn Mawr, I had a friend who was a woman, who-- but none of-- one of the women had children, but most of them didn't. There were some married women, but they did not have children. So working and children and-- [LAUGHING] it was not typical.

INT: And where was your mother?

ER: My mother stayed in New York and had a very active practice. And luckily, she got to know her grandchildren. Yeah, three grandchildren. When my mother was-- I mean, this is sort of a sad story. She fell on her head in my house when she was-- she was practicing medicine when she was 86. She was still getting new babies, [LAUGHING] so-- at 86. And she fell on her head, and she lost all function but got 90% back, at rehab, and insisted on going back to her apartment. She wanted to continue practicing medicine. But in the meantime, my sister and I thought she should stop. So she retired and stayed in New York City, in her apartment, where she knew people. She was comfortable. And so it worked out fine, except that I had to go there all the time, especially when she got older.

INT: Mhm.

ER: Yeah. But she was very beloved by everybody.

INT: Can you tell us more about your husband, where his family was from?

ER: Well, the interesting part about my husband was when I first met him, as I said, we went square dancing. And then we met through common things afterwards. And we were walking down Madison Avenue, and he said, yeah, I'm from Belgium. I said, oh, I'm from Yugoslavia. And he said, when did you come? And I said, I came in 1942. He said, I came in 1941. And he said, I came on the GuinÃ©. The ship was called GuinÃ©. And I said, what? When we came on the GuinÃ©, because that was a ship we came on, it was in all the newspapers. I forgot to tell you that. When we came, the news-- the media was there to take pictures and to write articles because our ship-- the war had started here-- our ship was the last ship they allowed to come in with refugees-- the last ship. So I have the articles. I have two of them. I couldn't track down others. And people around the country saw Dr. Erna Konig with this-- Dr. Erna Konig, there's probably nobody else named like that. And so they started calling. They heard that we were here. So people got in touch with her, because they read the papers. And it was syndicated around the country. But anyway, so my husband-- my boyfriend before-- no, he wasn't even a boyfriend at that point. We talked, and we had so much in common. His mother was a physician. My mother was a physician. His father was an engineer. My father had been, before he died, an engineer. They came out of Belgium as the Germans were strafing the lines of cars coming out of Belgium. We did not escape like that. I mean, they escaped, actually, and went to Portugal and stayed there for about a year, I think. But anyway, we had so much in common. So of course, we started dating, and that was that. Then, we got married after my freshman year. But, you know, I knew him for five years by the time I got married-- four years. Yeah. So he had a very, very similar background.

INT: It's a good start.

ER: And he was an engineer, and he was-- I think he was the youngest general manager at GE.

INT: Mhm.

ER: But he-- you know, he did pretty well. But we got divorced. [LAUGHING] You know, you change. Life changes you.

INT: OK. Is he still living?

ER: He died of COVID, in 2000.

INT: And where are your children living? Their names and where they're living?

ER: Jude lives-- she moved here when her daughter was in third grade, lived here 17 years. And right before COVID, they sold their house and moved to Germantown, New York on the Hudson. Dina-- as she said, she's a filmmaker. And Dina and the twins, she lives outside of Boston, in Wayland. And she's a psychologist. She's a PhD, psychologist, and has an active practice. David, her twin brother, lives in Portland-- outside of Portland, Maine, in Cape Elizabeth. And he's a psychiatrist. And Jean lives in Silicon Valley area, in the Bay Area near San Francisco. And she has twins, also, two girls, who started college now. So they all have kids. They're all married, and [PAUSES] it's-- we're very close. Oh, we-- something-- my sister and I and-- oh, I forget when it was. Oh, when she was-- maybe it was 12 or 13 years ago, took our families to Croatia. And my mother, my sister and I, the three of us came to the United States. And my sister and I took just our families, our kids and grandkids. We were 24, [LAUGHING] gone, went back. And so I organized the trip with my daughter, and we had an amazing time. We saw my house. My grandparents' house had this wall, whitewashed wall on their store. The house that the store was in had many apartments. My mother's office was in that-- one of the apartments and so on and so forth. On the wall said, "Garderobe Koenig," which means Koenig's store. Koenig was the name of the store. My father, that was their-- my mother's name. And that was still you could see it a little bit on the wall. So that was very interesting when we were there. Everybody got a kick out of that one.

INT: Yeah. Can you tell me about your professional career, please?

ER: Yeah, I was-- got my PhD in biochemistry and got a postdoc at University of Pennsylvania and worked there for two years as a postdoc. Then, I became a research associate, and then I got a job on the faculty, and I was a professor at the Medical College of Pennsylvania teaching biochemistry. My research was always in lipids. At first, it was on tetrahymena, which is a protozoan. And then, when I went to Penn, it was on herpesvirus, which has a lipid membrane. And when I went to MCP, which is Medical College of Pennsylvania, it was on atherosclerosis, but my part was on cholesterol metabolism. And when I was there about eight years-- oh, no, I was there about six years-- they asked me to be Dean of Women. I knew nothing about women. I mean, because I had four kids. I went to school. I worked full time. My mother was a doctor. I didn't realize women had problems or anything. So why do you need to-- it was a women's college, originally. They have a terrific archives for women. And they said, because you did it. I said, what do you mean? I don't know much about it. You worked. You had-- you did what women should do or want to do or whatever. So I became Dean of Women, including doing the biochemical research for a while. And I learned a lot about women. And I didn't know what kind of trouble I was facing all the time. You know, I mean, I didn't realize that it was difficult for women. Because it was difficult for me, but I thought it was the way it is. So then when I became Dean of Women and did the research, people started asking me to do some consulting on cell growth, because biotech was starting. How do you-- how do you increase the size of your cultures to manufacture things? So then I started doing consulting, and I was asked to form a consulting group. So my partner and I formed a group, and I did a lot of consulting for NASA, a lot. Because he worked for GE in the space program, so he got us jobs. I didn't. I worked for Sharon in Israel once, who was-- he was the minister-- the Sharon that you know about, but he was-- at that time, he was Minister of Industry and Trade. So he asked to have his technology world assessed, to decide where they should put their money, what's commercializable and what's worthwhile to follow. And so I did the, of course, the biomedical stuff, biotech, and so on, and my partner did all the physical electronic stuff. So we wrote reports for him. So I spent a lot of time in Israel. I loved Israel. I think I would have been so happy to have gone there. So--

INT: Me too.

ER: So I did a lot of interesting consulting.

INT: As you were very highly educated-- you were-- you traveled. You met people from all over the world, and you grew up as a refugee, initially. Would you like to comment about what's been going on in Ukraine and Israel?

ER: Oh, my god. My heart goes out to Ukrainian people. I can't imagine how horrible it is. I mean, I think I feel what everybody else does who has any kind of a heart. And I think what's happening in Israel right now is very, very sad. I mean, my heart breaks for the Palestinians and what they're doing to the Palestinians. I just-- I'm not that knowledgeable. I'm political here, very political. But I just don't know how-- what can be done. I really-- Israel has to continue. It has to exist. And I see no other way besides having a two-state--

INT: Two-state solution.

ER: Two-state solution. [PAUSES] I-- but I-- I just can't imagine. I can't imagine the nice people I know in Israel killing, doing all this killing. It's got to be another way. Well, I blame a lot of what we're hearing and how people here feel on the news media. I just don't think that they're fair in the way they report things. First of all, Israel has taken over and we don't hear much about the Ukraine anymore. It's all Israel, and it's all-- and it's all what Israel is doing. They don't talk about the fact that the Palestinians were bombing now for 70 years, that they were living under a regime-- they're living next to regimes whose mission is to get rid of Israel. If somebody was trying to get rid of my family, I would do anything to stop it. So that's one side of what I feel. The other side is, how can you hurt people who are innocent like that, Palestinians? How can they do that to the people? I don't know what the answers are. I don't know. My heart breaks.

INT: Do you think that you're particularly sensitive because of the way you were a victim of the war?

ER: Well, that's another story. [PAUSES] I don't think I'm very sensitive because of that. In fact, I think I'm the opposite because I think I just never thought about it. I use denial. I didn't worry about it. I'm sure I've got PTSD in some area of my life but certainly not sensitivity. Because I can compare myself to my sister, who is very sensitive. She could never watch Holocaust movies. I did. I mean, I can shut things out. And that's because of the war. I was much older, and I was able to develop defenses. She wasn't. That's very different.

INT: You mentioned that you're active politically. Do you want to explain that?

ER: Oh, I'm an elected committee person here. I represent our area in the county. I'm on the executive board of the county, which is elected by the executive board here-- you know, by the committee people here. And so I'm very active. My-- the mission-- our mission, basically, is to get people out to vote. I mean, get Democrats out to vote. So I'm a staunch Democrat. I can't say I'm all the way left-wing Democrat, but I'm definitely liberal-progressive in every way that I know of. [LAUGHING] And I'd never even read the papers until much, much later in life because I was too busy. And I knew my husband would tell me if anything was something I should know. And he did. He always talked about the news. So I didn't have time to read the papers. I read scientific stuff all the time that I had to read. And so then, I decided I have to do something for the world. And I am interested in politics. Because of my background, I'm very interested. And now, of course, I'm petrified, because I feel I'm living again in the 19-- I didn't live in the-- you know, I didn't know anybody in the '30s, but I'm scared for everybody. And I think people are so ignorant. I don't care if I say it [LAUGHING] on this thing. I don't understand how they're thinking. I just don't get it. Where do they come from? How can they vote for somebody who's a criminal, who's got 19-- what does he have?

